    73	The dynamic nature of Python also presents challenges when it comes to program analysis, error
    74	detection, security assessment, and performance optimization. Various dynamic program analysis
    75	techniques have been developed to address these challenges, e.g., to detect bugs [ 66 ], to enforce
    76	differential privacy [ 1 ], to slice programs [ 15 ], or to understand the performance of a program
    77	via profiling [7]. These techniques play a crucial role in identifying programming errors, security
    78	vulnerabilities, and performance bottlenecks. To help develop, evaluate, and compare dynamic
    79	analysis techniques, other popular programming languages have benchmark suites of executable
    80	code. For example, DaCapo for Java [ 10 ], SPEC CPU for C/C++ [ 31 ], and Da Capo con Scala [ 58 ]
    81	have contributed significantly to work by both researchers and practitioners on their respective
    82	programming languages.
    83	Despite the popularity and importance of Python, there currently is no equivalent benchmark
    84	suite for Python, which hinders the progress and evaluation of dynamic analyses. Instead, researchers, who want to work on a novel dynamic program analysis for Python, would like to
    85	study the runtime behavior of Python code, or want to gather data from executions of Python
    86	software, must spend significant efforts on creating a suitable set of executable programs. As a
    87	result, many evaluations that involve dynamic analysis of Python are performed on a relatively
    88	small set of benchmark programs, e.g., nine projects [ 21 ], five projects [ 59 ], six projects [ 16 ], and
    89	eleven projects [ 66 ]. Moreover, since every team working on Python creates their own benchmark,
    90	it is difficult to compare different techniques and results with each other.
    91	In response to this gap, this paper presents DyPyBench, a novel benchmark of executable Python
    92	projects. We envision our benchmark to facilitate future work on dynamically analyzing Python.
    93	To this end, the benchmark is the first to offer four important properties – large-scale, diverse,
    94	ready-to-run, and ready-to-analyze – as described in the following:
    95	
    96	
    97	  - Large-scale. To offer users an extensive collection of executable code, the benchmark includes
    98	a substantial number of popular and non-trivial projects. The definition of “large-scale” may
    99	vary depending on the specific use case. In this work, we have curated a dataset comprising
   100	50 projects, collectively amounting to 681k lines of code. This size is an order of magnitude
   101	larger than the benchmarks considered in previous evaluations, as listed above.
   102	
   103	  - Diverse. Given Python’s numerous applications, it is critical that a benchmark covers a
   104	wide number of application domains, which each have their own code style and libraries.
   105	DyPyBench attempts to represent the breadth and diversity of Python usage by including
   106	projects systematically sampled from different application domains, enabling extensive
   107	assessments and studies of code behavior across different contexts.
   108	
   109	  - Ready-to-run. Executing the code of a real-world, complex project is non-trivial, as it requires
   110	a suitable project configuration, installing third-party dependencies, and inputs that exercise
   111	the code. The effort spent on setting up projects can be significant, especially for users who
   112	are not familiar with the project, and usually, this effort needs to be spent again and again for
   113	each new project. DyPyBench comes with all projects already set up and uses the test suites
   114	of these projects to provide inputs. To facilitate running the code irrespective of variations in
   115	project dependencies, configurations, and execution environments, DyPyBench provides a
   116	unified interface that allows users to run the benchmark with a single command. Moreover, we
   117	provide a Docker image that encapsulates the benchmark and all its dependencies, ensuring
   118	reproducibility and longevity.
   119	
   120	  - Ready-to-analyze. To facilitate the dynamic analysis of the projects in DyPyBench, the
   121	benchmark integrates with DynaPyt [ 21 ], a general-purpose dynamic analysis framework
   122	for Python. The integration allows for instrumenting and analyzing projects with a single
