   131	command, making it straightforward to apply a new dynamic analysis to a wide range of
   132	projects.
   133	
   134	
   135	To evaluate the usefulness and practicality of DyPyBench, we perform a series of experiments
   136	covering a wide range of research areas. In particular, we apply our benchmark in three usage
   137	scenarios:
   138	
   139	
   140	(1) We use DyPyBench to empirically compare statically and dynamically created call graphs. To
   141	this end, we use the dynamic analysis infrastructure built into DyPyBench to gather dynamic
   142	call graphs and compare them to the results of a state of the art static call graph generator [ 54 ].
   143	Our results help understand the strengths and weaknesses of both approaches, and provide
   144	some guidelines for future work on call graph construction.
   145	(2) We use DyPyBench to create training data for LExecutor [ 59 ], a recent neural network-based
   146	technique for learning-guided execution. In this scenario, we apply the existing source code
   147	instrumentation tool provided by LExecutor to the code in DyPyBench and then execute our
   148	benchmark. By using the resulting 436,355 data examples as training data for the LExecutor
   149	model, we find that more data helps improve the accuracy of the model.
   150	(3) Finally, we use DyPyBench to mine specifications from execution traces. For this application,
   151	we again build upon the dynamic analysis support built into our benchmark, this time to
   152	extract traces of function calls. DyPyBench provided thousands of sequences of calls that
   153	allowed to extract some meaningful patterns, which sets a baseline for future work on
   154	dynamic specification mining for Python.
   155	
   156	For all three of these applications, one would usually have to spend a significant effort on setting up
   157	suitable projects and finding inputs, e.g., in the form of test suites, for exercising their code. Instead,
   158	DyPyBench provides this setup, which hugely facilitates these and future dynamic analyses for
   159	Python.
   160	In summary our work contributes the following:
   161	
   162	
   163	  - We address the lack of a comprehensive benchmark suite of executable Python projects by
   164	creating DyPyBench.
   165	
   166	  - We integrate our benchmark with the general-purpose dynamic analysis framework DynaPyt [ 21 ], which allows for performing arbitrary dynamic analyses on the entire benchmark
   167	with minimal effort.
   168	
   169	  - We illustrate the usefulness of DyPyBench in three application scenarios.
   170	
   171	
   172	**2** **METHODOLOGY**
   173	
   174	This section describes our methodology for creating a benchmark of executable Python software.
   175	We begin by presenting the criteria for selecting projects to include in the benchmark, and then
   176	describe our methodology for making these projects ready-to-execute and ready-to-analyze.
   177	
   178	
   179	**2.1** **Selecting Projects**
   180	
   181	To ensure the diversity and representativeness of the benchmark, we select projects from the
   182	Awesome Python repository, [2] which is a curated list of popular, open-source Python projects. The
   183	Awesome Python repository has 173k stars on GitHub and more than 400 contributors, which are
   184	indicators of its popularity and adoption by the community. The curated list contains 679 projects,
   185	including libraries, frameworks, and applications, which are classified into 90 categories.
   186	We select a subset of the projects from the curated list, following three criteria designed to ensure
   187	the diversity, quality, and representativeness of our benchmark. First, to increase the quality and
   188	
   189	
   190	2 [https://github.com/vinta/awesome-python](https://github.com/vinta/awesome-python)
   191	
   192	
   193	Proc. ACM Softw. Eng., Vol. 1, No. FSE, Article 16. Publication date: July 2024.
   194	
   195	
   196	16:4 Islem Bouzenia, Bajaj Piyush Krishan, and Michael Pradel
   197	
   198	
   199	relevance of the benchmark, we consider only projects that have at least 500 stars on their respective
   200	GitHub repositories. GitHub stars serve as an indication of a project’s popularity, reflecting its
   201	usefulness and community support. Second, to make DyPyBench diverse, we sample projects
   202	from different categories in the Awesome Python list. Each category covers a different application
   203	domain, such as web crawling, machine learning, and robotics. We randomly sample from all
   204	projects in the Awesome Python list, with the constraint to pick at most one project from each
   205	category. Finally, as our goal is to create an executable benchmark, we focus on projects with test
   206	suites. Specifically, we focus on test suites that can be executed with pytest, i.e., one of the most
   207	popular testing frameworks for Python. The selected tests include tests written based on Python’s
   208	built-in `unittest` framework, as well as tests written with pytest itself.
   209	Alternative to sampling projects from the Awesome Python list, we could have selected the most
   210	downloaded projects from the Python Package Index (PyPI). However, we observe that the most
   211	downloaded projects do not fully represent the diversity of the Python ecosystem, but are biased
   212	toward kinds of projects that many others depend on, such as tools to build and set up projects.
   213	Instead, the Awesome Python list provides an independently curated list of projects grouped into a
   214	diverse set of application domains. A downside of using the Awesome Python list is that it focuses
   215	on open-source projects only, and hence, may not be fully representative of the entire Python
   216	ecosystem. We decided to focus on open-source projects, as they are more likely to be used in
   217	research and are more accessible to the community.
   218	
   219	
   220	**2.2** **Enabling Execution**
   221	As executability is an important property of our benchmark, we invest significant efforts into
   222	automating the process of setting up the projects and their test suites. Our benchmark can be
   223	set up in two ways. First, we provide a Docker image that encapsulates the benchmark and all
   224	its dependencies. This image can be used to run the benchmark on any machine that supports
   225	Docker, ensuring reproducibility and longevity. Second, we provide a command-line interface
   226	that allows users to automatically install and set up the benchmark and its projects. This process
   227	involves cloning the projects, installing their dependencies, and configuring their test suites. To
   228	the extent possible, DyPyBench invokes standard Python tools for installing, building, and testing
   229	projects. However, some projects require various non-standard steps, such as installing unspecified
   230	third-party dependencies or slightly adapting the developer-provided test suites. To address these
   231	cases, we manually inspect the projects and make the necessary adjustments to ensure that they are
   232	ready-to-execute. From the perspective of a user of our benchmark, this process is transparent, as
   233	the benchmark provides a single command for setting up and executing all or individual projects.
   234	The following describes the steps of our automated setup process in detail. At first, we clone the
   235	project repositories from GitHub. To ensure reproducibility, we clone the repositories at a specific
   236	commit, which is the latest commit on or before January 18, 2023. Then, we create a Python virtual
   237	environment for each project, which ensures that the project’s dependencies are installed in an
   238	isolated manner. Next, we install the project’s dependencies within the virtual environment. If
   239	available, this step uses the project’s `requirements.txt` file, which we augment with additional
   240	dependencies if necessary. Finally, we install the project itself, ensuring that it is properly set up
   241	within its dedicated virtual environment.
   242	To ensure that the test suites of the projects are ready-to-execute, we configure them to run with
   243	pytest. This step involves specifying the locations of the test cases within the project. For some
   244	projects, additional dependencies may be required to execute the test suite. These dependencies
   245	are often not mentioned in the requirements file but can<response clipped><NOTE>To save on context only part of this file has been shown to you. You should retry this tool after you have searched inside the file with `grep -n` in order to find the line numbers of what you are looking for.</NOTE>


