     3	[<img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/dockerhub-DyPyBench-blue.svg?logo=Docker">](https://hub.docker.com/r/islemdockerdev/dypybench)
     4	
     5	The first benchmark of Python projects that is large-scale, diverse, ready-to-run (i.e., with fully configured and prepared test suites), and
     6	ready-to-analyze (i.e., using an integrated Python dynamic analysis framework). The benchmark encompasses
     7	50 popular open-source projects from various application domains, with a total of 681K lines of Python code,
     8	and 30K test cases.
     9	
    10	**For more information, check our paper:
    11	https://www.software-lab.org/publications/fse2024_dypybench.pdf**
    12	
    13	## 1. Using the Benchmark
    14	
    15	Before downloading and using the Docker image of DyPyBench, please check the requirements [here](./REQUIREMENTS.md)
    16	
    17	**Important Note:** 
    18	
    19	As the image size is 55GB, we could not put it on zenodo because they only allow up to 50GB. 
    20	However, we put scripts to reproduce the paper's experiments and the obtained data on zenodo alongside the scripts required to rebuild DyPyBench from scratch.
    21	zenodo Zip File: https://zenodo.org/records/10683759
    22	
    23	### Step 1. Fetching the image from DockerHub
    24	
    25	1. Pull the docker image from dockerhub
    26	    - docker pull islemdockerdev/dypybench:v2.0
    27	2. Run the docker image to start the container
    28	    - docker run -itd --name dypybench islemdockerdev/dypybench:v2.0
    29	3. Login to the container
    30	    - docker start -i dypybench
    31	
    32	Important complementary Action: **Apply the most recent patch**
    33	
    34	Check below on how to do it. (See within this file, the section: 4. Maintainance and Future Support)
    35	
    36	### Step 2. Interacting with the Command Line
    37	Here is a list of the most useful commands of DyPyBench.
    38	
    39	1. List the projects setup in DyPyBench
    40	    - python3 dypybench.py --list
    41	2. Run Test Suites of one or more available projects
    42	    - python3 dypybench.py --test 1 2 3 4
    43	    - Example: python3 dypybench.py --test 1
    44	2. Run DynaPyt Instrumentation
    45	    - python3 dypybench.py --dynapyt_instrument 1 2 3 4 --dynapyt_file ./text/includes.txt --dynapyt_analysis TraceAll
    46	    - Example: python3 dypybench.py --dynapyt_instrument 1 --dynapyt_file ./text/includes.txt --dynapyt_analysis TraceAll
    47	3. Run DynaPyt Analysis
    48	    - python3 dypybench.py --dynapyt_run 1 2 3 4 --dynapyt_analysis TraceAll
    49	    - Example: python3 dypybench.py --dynapyt_run 1 --dynapyt_analysis TraceAll
    50	4. Run LExecutor Instrumentation
    51	    - python3 dypybench.py --lex_instrument 1 2 3 4 --lex_file ./text/includes.txt
    52	    - Example: python3 dypybench.py --lex_instrument 1 --lex_file ./text/includes.txt
    53	5. Run tests to generate LExecutor trace
    54	    - python3 dypybench.py --lex_test 1 2 3 4
    55	    - Example: python3 dypybench.py --lex_test 1
    56	6. Run PyCG
    57	    - python3 dypybench.py --pycg 1 2 3 4
    58	    - Example: python3 dypybench.py --pycg 1
    59	7. Update DynaPyt source code
    60	    - python3 dypybench.py --update_dynapyt_source
    61	8. Update LExecutor source code
    62	    - python3 dypybench.py --update_lex_source
    63	
    64	Alongside the commands above, you can have more control on some commands using the following list of available flags with explanation:
    65	
    66	#### Available flags
    67	1. --list / -l 
    68	    - List the projects
    69	2. --test / -t
    70	    - Specify projects for test
    71	3. --dynapyt_instrument / -di
    72	    - Specify projects for DynaPyt instrumentation
    73	4. --dynapyt_run / -dr
    74	    - Specify projects for DynaPyt analysis 
    75	5. --dynapyt_file / -df
    76	    - Specify path of includes.txt file for DynaPyt instrumentation
    77	6. --dynapyt_analysis / -da
    78	    - Specify name of the DynaPyt analysis to run
    79	7. --save / -s
    80	    - Specify the file to save output
    81	8. --test_original / -to
    82	    - Run tests on code present in original folder
    83	9. --update_dynapyt_source
    84	    - Get or update DynaPyt source code
    85	10. --update_lex_source
    86	    - Get or update LExecutor source code
    87	11. --lex_instrument / -li
    88	    - Specify the project no. to run LExecutor instrumentation
    89	12. --lex_file / -lf
    90	    - Specify the path to file containing the includes.txt file to run LExecutor instrumentation
    91	13. --lex_test / -lt
    92	    - Specify the project no. to run LExecutor for trace generation
    93	14. --timeout
    94	    - Specify timeout to be used in seconds for running test suite and analysis
    95	15. --pycg / -scg
    96	    - Specify project to generate static call graphs using PyCG
    97	
    98	#### Useful commands to copy files from and into the Docker image
    99	1. Using volume to map local directory to container directory
   100	    - Start the container with the --volume flag and provide full folder paths
   101	        - docker run -itd --volume local_folder:container_folder --name dypybench dypybench/dypybench:v1.0
   102	2. Copy files or folders individually from running container to local machine
   103	    - docker cp container_name:container_path local_path 
   104	3. Copy files or folders individually to running container from local machine
   105	    - docker cp local_path container_name:container_path
   106	
   107	## 2. Reproducing the Experiments of the Paper
   108	For the ones interested, we also provide the notebooks (and intermediate data) used in the analysis presented the overview (Sec3) and analysis (Sec4) sections of our paper. Please find instructions on how to reproduce in [experiments/README.md](experiments/README.md).
   109	
   110	General requirements can be found [here](./REQUIREMENTS.md).
   111	
   112	Specific Python requirments can be found [here](./experiments/requirements.txt).
   113	
   114	
   115	## 3. OPTIONAL: Build DyPyBench from Scratch
   116	
   117	### Requirements
   118	    - Python >= 3.8
   119	    - pip >= 22.0
   120	    - python3-virtualenv >= 20.16.6
   121	    - ffmpeg (project requirement)
   122	    - libjpeg8-dev (project requirement)
   123	    - libavcodec-extra (project requirement)
   124	    - Git >= 2.34
   125	    - Docker >= 20.10
   126	
   127	### Steps
   128	1. Clone DyPyBench Repository
   129	    - git clone this repo
   130	2. Build docker image using docker build command
   131	    - docker build -t dypybench .
   132	3. Run the created docker image to start the container
   133	    - docker run -itd --name dypybench dypybench
   134	4. Login to the docker container and execute the bash scripts.
   135	    - docker start -i dypybench
   136	    - ./scripts/install-all-projects.sh > install.log 2>&1
   137	
   138	## 4. Maintainance and Future Support
   139	Due to the substantial size of the image (55GB), uploading a new image for minor changes is impractical. Consequently, we propose implementing a patching mechanism as follows:
   140	
   141	- Patches addressing specific issues will appear in the "Releases" section of this repository; they will be provided as zip files.
   142	    
   143	- Copy the patch zip file into the Docker container and proceed to unzip it
   144	
   145	    ```bash
   146	    # Copy the patch to the docker image
   147	    docker cp patch_XX.zip dypybench:/DyPyBench
   148	    # Inside your docker, in the directory /DyPyBench run this command
   149	    unzip patch_XX.zip
   150	    ```
   151	    
   152	- Execute the command "python3.10 patch_XX.py" within the container, replacing "patch_XX.py" with the accurate script name obtained after unzipping the patch.
   153	    ```bash
   154	    # move to the patch directory
   155	    cd patch_XX
   156	    # execute the patch script
   157	    python3.10 patch_XX.py
   158	    ```
   159	
   160	### The list of important patches (so far):
   161	* [patch_2.1](https://github.com/sola-st/DyPyBench/releases/tag/v2.1.0)
   162	
