   810	**5** **RELATED WORK**
   811	
   812	
   813	Dynamic analysis and testing of Python. Python has been the target of several dynamic analyses,
   814	e.g., to slice programs [ 15 ], to detect type-related bugs [ 66 ], and to enforce differential privacy [ 1 ].
   815	Scalene [ 7 ] offers an efficient CPU and memory profiler. DyPyBench can serve as a dataset to test and
   816	evaluate these and future dynamic analyses. Pynguin [ 38 ] is a unit-level, search-based test generator
   817	for Python. LExecutor [ 59 ] proposes learning-guided execution, which executes code by injecting
   818	otherwise missing runtime values. Both Pynguin and LExecutor share with DyPyBench the goal of
   819	executing code. DyPyBench complements these existing efforts by providing a reproducible setup
   820	that allows for executing and analyzing a set of real-world projects with little effort. DynaPyt [ 21 ]
   821	is a general-purpose dynamic analysis framework. Because it allows for implementing arbitrary
   822	dynamic analyses, we integrate DynaPyt into this work and show two applications of it (Sections 4.1
   823	and 4.3).
   824	
   825	
   826	Other work on Python. Beyond dynamic analysis, the increasing importance of Python has lead
   827	to a stream of work on studying Python software and on supporting Python developers. A study
   828	of flaky tests [ 27 ] relates to this work because they try to automatically execute the test suites of
